# Udemy Transcript + Textbook Documentation

When this rule is applied or the user provides a Udemy lecture URL (or has one open), do **all** of these every time:

1. Save the **transcript** to `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/`
2. Use the **course images** the user placed in `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/`
3. Write **textbook documentation** to `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/`
4. Add or update the lecture link on the landing page `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`

Use `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/sample.md` for **writing style** (novice prose, `- [x]` bullets, exam-style Q&A). Do **not** copy its old collapsible **Terminal Commands** / **Code** layout. Do **not** write lecture content into `01.md`. `01.md` is only the landing page + table of contents. Manual archive is `Personal/Files/aws/old.md`.

## A. Extract the transcript

1. Navigate to the lecture URL (`browser_navigate`) or use the open tab.
2. Snapshot the page (`browser_snapshot`).
3. Open the **Transcript** panel if it is closed (transcript toggle / `data-purpose="transcript-toggle"`).
4. Collect cue lines in order (`role: button` names between video controls and **Autoscroll**).
5. Get the lecture title and **sequence number** from course content (example: `Play 2. The SAA-C03 Exam` -> `2`).
6. Save `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/notes/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.txt`:

```
Lecture: <title>
URL: <url>
============================================================

<transcript lines>
```

Filename rules: spaces to `-`, keep readable Title-Case, drop special characters except hyphens.

## B. Use the course images the user provides

Do **not** screenshot the Udemy video player. The lecture stream is DRM-encrypted; seek-preview frames are black or unusable.

1. Look in `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/images/<sequence>/`.
2. Keep the user’s filenames (lecture 3 style: `1.png`, `2.png`, …).
3. If that folder is missing or empty, **stop** and ask the user to drop the extracted course stills there before writing the lecture page.
4. Use **every** image in that folder. Embed each one next to the transcript content it illustrates.
5. From the lecture `.md` file in `udemy/md/`, the path is `../images/<sequence>/<filename>` (example: `../images/3/1.png`).
6. Caption as **Figure N.** plus a short description of what the slide or UI actually shows. Do not invent extra figures that are not in the folder.

Example for lecture 3:

- `../images/3/1.png` — title slide
- `../images/3/2.png` — Free Tier vs Sandbox comparison
- `../images/3/3.png` — Challenge Labs website
- `../images/3/4.png` — Challenge Lab console + instructions

## C. Write textbook documentation

Create `Personal/Files/aws/udemy/md/<sequence>-<Hyphenated-Title>.md`. Every lecture page has **exactly these sections**, in this order:

1. Title heading with lecture number
2. Course + lecture links + transcript path
3. **Introduction** (prose a novice can follow)
4. **Detailed Explanation** grouped into steps (see below)
5. **Lab** (a real walkthrough for HOL/console lessons; for theory lessons, say there is no console lab and keep any study snippets here too)
6. **Questions and Answers** (exam-focused; keep each **Answer** in a collapsible `<details>`)
7. **Summary**
8. **References** (Udemy lecture, official AWS docs, transcript file)

Do **not** create standalone **Terminal Commands** or **Code** sections.

### Which sections are accordions

Keep these headings **open** (no outer `<details>`): **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References**.

Wrap these in `<details>` accordions:

- Each **Step** inside Detailed Explanation (one accordion per step)
- The whole **Lab** section
- The whole **Questions and Answers** section (individual **Answer** blocks stay nested `<details>` inside it)

Do **not** wrap command or code snippets in their own nested accordions. They stay visible inside the Step or Lab that uses them.

### Detailed Explanation must use the Step format

This is the house style for **every** lecture page. Never write `## Detailed Explanation` as one flat list of bullets.

1. Group the transcript's sub-topics into **4–7 steps** (up to ~10 for a very long cram/cheat-sheet lecture).
2. Head each group with `### Step N — <short descriptive title>`, using an em dash `—` with a space on each side, sentence case, no trailing period.
3. Steps run `1..N` in transcript order, with no gaps or duplicates.
4. Put **each** step in its own accordion: `<details>` / `<summary>Step N — title</summary>` / the `### Step N — title` heading and its body / `</details>`.
5. Under each step, keep the `- [x]` bullets (and their nested children) taken from the transcript.
6. Place a figure inside the step whose bullets it illustrates, after those bullets.

Example shape:

```
## Detailed Explanation

<details>
  <summary>Step 1 — Place SAA-C03 on the AWS certification ladder</summary>

### Step 1 — Place SAA-C03 on the AWS certification ladder

- [x] **What SAA-C03 is**
  - Exam code for **AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate**.

</details>

<details>
  <summary>Step 2 — Learn the logistics: time, questions, and exam-day tactics</summary>

### Step 2 — Learn the logistics: time, questions, and exam-day tactics

- [x] **Length, question count, and style**
  - **130 minutes** and **65 questions**.

</details>
```

Lab and Q&A use the same accordion pattern:

```
<details>
  <summary>Lab</summary>

## Lab

...

</details>

<details>
  <summary>Questions and Answers</summary>

## Questions and Answers

### Question 1: ...

<details>
<summary>Answer</summary>

- [x] ...

</details>

</details>
```

Reference pages: `udemy/md/1-Welcome-and-How-to-Use-this-Course.md`, `udemy/md/2-The-SAA-C03-Exam.md`, `udemy/md/3-Hands-On-Practice-Free-Tier-vs-Sandbox.md`.

### Terminal commands and code live in the step and the lab

Never isolate commands or snippets in their own section. When a lecture uses a CLI command, JSON policy, ARN, URL, or other snippet:

1. Put the fenced block **inside the Detailed Explanation step** that teaches it (after the bullets that introduce it).
2. Put the **same** snippet again **inside the Lab** task (or Lab overview, for theory lessons) that actually uses it.

Show the snippet inline. Do **not** hide it behind a nested `<details>` accordion. If a lesson has no commands and no code, write none — do not add placeholder `# No commands in this topic` blocks.

For HOL/console lessons, write a real step-by-step tutorial a beginner can repeat. For theory lessons, say there is no console lab and keep Q&A exam-focused.

## D. Update the landing page

In `Personal/Files/aws/01.md`, add the new lecture under the correct section in **Table of contents**:

```
N. [Lecture Title](udemy/md/N-Hyphenated-Title.md)
```

Do not duplicate an existing TOC row. Do not put full lecture notes in `01.md`.

## Confirm

Tell the user: transcript path + line count, doc path, figure list from `udemy/images/<sequence>/`, and that `01.md` was updated.

